# time.manifest — config owned by the NTP subsystem (w-time).
#
# Layered (update-system.md phase 3):
#   • /usr/share/w/defaults/time.conf   — W's NTP catalog + default selection.
#     Class managed: overwritten by every update, so a new server set ships to
#     installed machines. Entries MERGE per key — an operator-added NTP_<name>
#     lives in the admin file below and survives updates on its own.
#   • /usr/share/w/defaults/time.schema — who may set each key (system-scope:
#     there is one system clock, a per-user override would be meaningless).
#   • /etc/w/time.conf — this machine's DEVIATIONS only (the active selection once
#     it differs from W's default, plus any site-specific NTP_<name>). Class
#     override: `w-time servers <name>` rewrites it at runtime and the update never
#     touches it; `w-reset time` drops the deviations back to the vendor layer.
#
# The rendered /etc/systemd/timesyncd.conf.d/10-w-ntp.conf is w-time output
# (regenerate with `sudo w-time apply`), not tracked here. Timezone and the NTP
# on/off flag are live system state (timedatectl), not config.
#
# Format: <path>\t<class>   class: managed | override
/usr/share/w/defaults/time.conf	managed
/usr/share/w/defaults/time.schema	managed
/etc/w/time.conf	override
